WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.910
- Thanks to Indochino for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.333
and helping me look fly.

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:05.166
(eagle crying)

00:00:05.166 --> 00:00:06.290
The Senate impeachment trial

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:08.410
of President Donald Trump has begun,

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.880
and day one was filled with fireworks,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.080
bombshells, lies, and deception,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.700
things that bode well and
terribly for your case

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.080
whether you are a
Republican or a Democrat.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.840
There was something for everyone.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.090
So, let's dig in to all of the craziness

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.300
that happened on day one.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.680
Let's also find out why Senator Ted Cruz

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.782
apparently is singling
me out for trolling.

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:31.532
(dramatic music)

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.900
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.100
because the first day of the
impeachment trial in the Senate

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.400
was filled with all kinds of lawyer skills

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.070
and complete sophistry.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:45.480
So, let's dig in to some of
the more important things

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.190
that happened during the first day,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.920
because a lot has changed,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.010
and it's going to set the tone

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.190
for the rest of the impeachment trial.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.330
The first day of the
trial was ostensibly about

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.310
setting the rules for
the rest of the trial,

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.790
and indeed, they did, in fact,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.440
set some ground rules for
the rest of the trial.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.630
Interestingly, in the
run-up to this first day

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.000
of the Senate trial,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.670
Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.450
had said that he was going
to pattern the proposed rules

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.060
off of what was used in
the impeachment trial,

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.190
the idea being that if it was good

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.610
for the trial of a Democratic president,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:20.310
it would be good for the trial

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.300
of a Republican president as well.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.650
The proposed rules had been held back,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.060
and it was only over the
weekend that we got to see

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.990
what the proposed rules
were going to look like.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.090
And interestingly,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.120
McConnell had made several key
changes to what was proposed

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.350
compared to what was used

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.170
during the Clinton impeachment trial.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.720
For instance, two of the
major key changes included

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.570
that all of the argument
would have to be compressed

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.560
into a 24-hour period
spread out over two days,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.450
and that the evidence that came

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.980
from the impeachment hearings in the House

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.890
would not be admitted automatically

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.630
into the Senate trial.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.940
Those were big deviations

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.090
compared to the Clinton-era rules.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:00.510
But interestingly, it
seems that those changes

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.240
were repealed at the last
minute after a meeting

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.740
with some of the more
moderate Senate Republicans.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.170
So now, the rules that were approved

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.920
on a strict party line vote

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.510
require that the argument be
made over a three-day period,

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.270
which is commensurate with what happened

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.860
in the Clinton trial,

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.890
but also that evidence from
the impeachment hearings

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.990
in the House can be admitted
into evidence in the Senate.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.230
However, with one big
caveat that is a deviation

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.290
from the Clinton trial,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.190
that the evidence is subject

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.660
to objections from the White
House or other senators,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.260
including hearsay,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.830
which is problematic, to say the least.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:37.860
Now, of course, the Senate can
make whatever rules it wants.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.400
It can adopt the Federal
Rules of Evidence or not.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.710
But what we've seen is
that there are plenty

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.350
of misunderstandings about
what hearsay actually means,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.750
and we've covered some of
those misunderstandings

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.870
on this channel.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.800
It's going to be problematic because here,

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.010
a lot of the testimony that is relevant

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.030
to the president's motivations
and the president's actions

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.070
are necessarily going to include things

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.310
that other people have said.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:01.760
Those things, for example,

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.540
what the president told Rudy
Giuliani or Mick Mulvaney.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.960
Those things are relevant
in and of themselves

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.010
as legal acts,

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.670
which would defeat an
argument that they're hearsay.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.910
But even things that Rudy
Giuliani told other people,

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.770
including potentially Lev Parnas

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.930
and other associates of
Giuliani and the president,

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.120
would potentially be considered hearsay,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:24.940
but they would be admissible in court

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:26.690
if it were an actual court

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.260
because they are
declarations against interest

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.690
or declarations of a party.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.890
There's all kinds of
reasons why even, quote,

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.880
hearsay evidence would come in.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.550
So, the carve-out is
particularly interesting

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:37.430
from a legal perspective,

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.280
but it is ripe for abuse,

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.130
because people simply don't understand,

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.550
sometimes willfully don't understand

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.690
the idea and concept of hearsay,

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.480
especially in this context.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.770
As I mentioned, it was a
straight party line vote,

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.850
53 Republicans, no defections,

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:55.150
to 47 Democrats, no defections.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.210
Now, not only did the
Senate procedural rules

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:58.710
cover the amount of time given

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:01.530
to both sides to make their
case and their defenses

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.960
and the admission of
evidence that was procured

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.230
during the House impeachment proceedings,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.090
but it also covered the new
evidence that will be added

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.370
to the Senate trial.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.530
Or more specifically,

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.630
it did not cover the evidence
that will be added to trial.

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:17.700
For all intents and purposes,

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.840
the Senate is kicking that
question down the road,

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.540
though that's not necessarily
the end of this story.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.450
The senators can always
pick up this question again

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.040
with some of the more moderate Republicans

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.010
arguing that they will
make a second decision

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.710
once they have heard all of the argument

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.240
from the House managers

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.780
and the president's defense attorneys.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.300
And that does comport with what happened

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.200
in the Clinton trial

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.210
where that decision was not made up front,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.320
but was made several weeks
into the Senate trial.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.350
Though, it's also
possible that the senators

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:47.740
will not take up this question again

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.970
and there will be no
additional new evidence

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.010
in the Senate trial.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.110
Which raises the specter

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.190
of what commentator Dahlia Lithwick

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:57.410
summarized the Senate GOP position as,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.640
it's too late for impeachment witnesses

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.440
and it's also too early
for impeachment witnesses.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.660
The Senate GOP has been arguing

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.080
that the witnesses should have been called

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.300
in the impeachment hearings in the House,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.830
and that it's also too early

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:14.820
to address that question in the Senate,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.370
and also by the way,

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.420
no witnesses should be allowed in either

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.100
the impeachment hearings in the House

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:23.460
or in the Senate impeachment trial either.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.710
And the Republicans argue at the same time

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.830
that the Democrats should
wait for the courts

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.190
to decide these questions
of the subpoenas themselves,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.910
and whether the witnesses
can be compelled to testify.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.570
At the same time, arguing that the courts

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.830
have absolutely no right
to decide these questions

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.790
because of separation of powers issues.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.950
It does often feel like the arguments

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.670
are that it's never the right time

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:49.020
and it's never the right forum
to decide these questions.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.220
Again, in strict party line votes,

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:53.130
the Senate Republicans voted to dismiss

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:56.440
11 amendments that Senate
minority leader Chuck Schumer

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.670
tried to add to the Senate rules,

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:00.600
each of which attempted to bring in

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:04.510
various kinds of evidence
into the Senate trial,

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:06.900
including subpoenas for documents

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.120
and witnesses from the White House.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.400
These amendments would
have approved subpoenas

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:12.840
for things like communications

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.800
between the White House and
Ukrainian President Zelensky,

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:18.430
documents and testimony from individuals

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.520
like acting Chief of Staff Mick Mulvaney,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:23.170
Secretary of State Mike Pompeo,

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:26.040
and former National Security
Advisor John Bolton.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.680
And in a really interesting gambit,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.020
one of the amendments would have allowed

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.750
Chief Justice Roberts to make the call

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.610
as to which subpoenas for
testimony and documents

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.760
would be approved.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.010
But again, along strict party line votes,

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:40.930
the Republicans voted not to allow

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:42.920
any new evidence to come in

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:44.470
and voted down an amendment

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.830
that would've vested the power to decide

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.330
whether new evidence would come in

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:51.230
with the chief justice
presiding over this trial.

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.400
The next set of fireworks
came from the Democrats,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.789
who came out swinging and
arguing substantive points

00:06:56.789 --> 00:06:58.570
on the first day of the trial.

00:06:58.570 --> 00:06:59.730
Now, by all accounts,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.840
it was thought that
everyone was simply going

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.600
to discuss the procedure
and procedural rules

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.890
that were going to govern
the rest of the Senate trial.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.930
The Democrats used their time
in their opening statements

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:13.730
to talk about the substantive
arguments for impeachment.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.470
Whether you like this tactic

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.650
or you found it to be
complete histrionics,

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:22.020
this is an interesting
and classic trial tactic,

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.530
to use all of the time that you have

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:28.110
allotted in front of the trier
of fact or the trier of law

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.440
to get your point across.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:31.960
This is something that happens

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:34.560
in lawsuits and trials all the time.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.220
I've seen it many times

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.120
where lawyers argue with a judge,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.520
digging straight into their arguments

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.860
when the judge doesn't
really have any idea

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.490
of what they're talking about.

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.870
Whereas if you give some background

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:51.740
and argue a lot of your
substantive points up front,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.520
then the more nuanced points
that you might make later on

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:55.960
will actually resonate with the judge,

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:58.020
who has a million things on their docket.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.730
And the tactics that Adam Schiff

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.280
and some of the other Democrats took

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.270
on the first day of the Senate trial

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:08.250
sort of hearkened back to
that particular trial tactic,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:13.250
that instead of simply arguing
the mere procedural points,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.920
which they eventually did,
especially with Chuck Schumer,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.150
they took that opportunity
to start making their case

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.900
in front of the Senators
and the American people.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.930
So, whether you liked that tactic or not,

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.770
whether you thought it
was pure grandstanding,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:27.603
or you thought the Democrats

00:08:27.603 --> 00:08:29.640
were making some good substantive points,

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.090
it has its basis in classic
litigation and trial tactics

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:36.300
to be able to get your story out

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:38.340
at every avenue that you can,

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:42.470
and not just be cabined into
the specific issue in front,

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:44.210
but to be able to talk
about all of the issues

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:45.160
at the same time.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.380
Because of course, if you have a theme,

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:49.970
you want to reiterate that
theme or that narrative

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:51.040
as many times as you can,

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:52.530
hoping that it will stick.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:53.810
And of course, it was interesting

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:55.880
to compare the Democratic narrative,

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:57.890
which went into the substantive issues,

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:01.770
with the narrative put out by
the lawyers for the president,

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.390
who it appears were prepared to argue

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:05.440
for the substantive points,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.320
but perhaps not all of the
substantive arguments on day one.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.290
Which leads me to the next
part of this impeachment trial

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:16.270
that really struck a chord
with me as a practicing lawyer,

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:20.350
was the marathon nature of
this particular proceeding.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:22.530
This may be a feature, not a bug.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.360
When you're in trial, the
judge sets the schedule,

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.900
and there's a reason why often,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:32.390
trial starts at 10:00 a.m.
and wraps up by 4:00 p.m.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.540
with maybe two or three
hours of substantive things

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.190
actually happening during the day.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:40.490
The reason is that the jury
will get fatigued over time.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:44.110
It's really, really hard
to be glued to your seat

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:47.160
for a full business day,

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.300
listening to all of these really complex

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:50.700
and arcane arguments.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.100
And of course, the same thing is in effect

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.610
with the Senate trial as well.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:58.310
This day one of the trial
went long into the night,

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:01.140
and unless you are a legal freak like me,

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.650
you probably had better
things to do during the day

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:08.650
than to watch 12 hours of
testimony and argument on C-SPAN.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.530
That has an effect.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.870
It's very clear that both sides
are going to get fatigued,

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:16.090
that the American people
are going to get fatigued,

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.740
and that cramming so much into one day,

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.730
it's gonna be hard to follow

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.630
all of the different
things that are happening,

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.030
and things are gonna get chopped up

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:27.048
into soundbites by both sides.

00:10:27.048 --> 00:10:28.750
It's gonna be really hard to pay attention

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.140
as the weeks go by and this continues.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.520
Now, one thing I thought
was particularly interesting

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.090
is that both sides, both
Republicans and Democrats,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.870
are arguing that these
impeachment proceedings

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:40.130
both in the House and the Senate

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:43.470
are precedent-shattering
and are hugely different

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.060
from everything that has come before.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.280
And in some respects, they're
definitely both right,

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:49.377
and one of the reasons for that,

00:10:49.377 --> 00:10:53.030
and one of the huge reasons
why the evidentiary issues

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.240
are so incredibly important in
these impeachment proceedings

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.730
compared to impeachment
proceedings in the past

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.410
is that there was no
independent investigation.

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:05.760
By necessity, the House had
to do the investigating,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.640
which it's really not set up to do

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.440
and is bound to get locked up

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:11.220
in separation of powers issues.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.270
In the only two other modern
examples of impeachments,

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.310
we had an independent counsel
doing the investigating.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.190
In the Nixon era, we had special
prosecutor Leon Jaworski,

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.510
who was able to do a
thorough investigation

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.300
into the Nixon affair,

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:27.680
including being able to
subpoena documents and testimony

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:28.513
from individuals,

00:11:28.513 --> 00:11:31.120
which admittedly went
up to the Supreme Court,

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.250
which forced the White House

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.140
to turn over the famous Nixon tapes.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:35.973
That's one of the reasons

00:11:35.973 --> 00:11:38.320
the investigation took years to complete.

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:39.430
In the Clinton era,

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:41.290
we had independent counsel Kenneth Starr,

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.420
who again, had the full
force of the executive branch

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:48.850
to do an investigation into
the various Clinton affairs.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.220
Often, when you're talking
about impeachment in the House,

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:53.400
the analogy is made to
the criminal context

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.680
where the impeachment is an indictment.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:57.560
There is some investigation,

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:00.230
but often, you are waiting
for additional evidence

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.650
to come out through
further investigations,

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.390
where the trial in the Senate

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:06.640
is like a criminal trial in real life

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.330
where it can use the
evidence that was found

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.120
in the House impeachment
indictment, so to speak,

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.910
like a grand jury investigation,

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:17.980
but more can come out during
the Senate trial itself.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.780
That analogy is probably a good one,

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:23.260
but in practice, what we have had is,

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.650
in the last two impeachment proceedings,

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.080
there was a thorough investigation done

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:30.570
before it ever got to
the trial in the Senate.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.630
And of course, when it
comes to the Nixon affair,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.480
it never even got to
the trial in the Senate.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.810
House manager Adam Schiff made this point

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:39.200
on the floor of the Senate

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.070
during the first day of the trial

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:44.270
when he said that the
Clinton impeachment trial

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.470
was more about recalling witnesses

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:50.060
than it was about calling
witnesses in the first place.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:52.010
So, regardless of which side of the aisle

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:53.560
you happen to stand,

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.190
this is very different,

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:57.240
and it may be different out of necessity

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.500
because we don't have
an independent counsel.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:02.700
One was not appointed by
Attorney General William Barr,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.840
and we don't have the same kinds of laws

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:06.510
that would have appointed
a special prosecutor

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.220
like Leon Jaworski to do the investigation

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:12.500
that the House necessarily
had to undertake,

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.970
which is very, very new.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:16.840
Of course, another legal bombshell came

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.160
actually from the
defenders of the president,

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:22.380
and may have severe repercussions
for ongoing litigation,

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:24.270
because the president's defenders argued

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:25.980
that the Democrats should have waited

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.660
for the courts to decide a lot
of these evidentiary issues

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.490
and procured more evidence in the House

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.100
before going to the trial in the Senate.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:37.530
The thing is, there's
active, ongoing litigation

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.960
over the subpoena to former
White House counsel Don McGahn.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.470
The White House has argued that the courts

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:47.330
have no business adjudicating
the White House's refusal

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:49.860
to turn over Don McGahn for testimony.

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.340
The DOJ as recently as last month

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.460
argued that courts can't adjudicate

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.370
the privileges used for
separation of powers issues.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.160
The thing is, on the floor of the Senate,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:00.810
the president' attorney Jay Sekulow

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:02.470
argued that Democrats rushed things

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:05.710
and should have waited for
the judiciary to weigh in.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:07.033
- The rush to impeachment,

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:11.370
to not wait for a decision from a court

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.600
on an issue as important
as executive privilege.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.170
The president's opponents,
in their rush to impeach,

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:18.580
have refused to wait for
complete judicial review.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:20.940
- Other lawyers for the
president are in court

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.590
saying the exact opposite of
what they're telling you today.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:27.630
They're saying you cannot
enforce congressional subpoenas,

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.120
that's nonjusticiable.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:33.150
- That is such a stunning
admission and contradiction that,

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:36.020
if I was one of the lawyers
working on that particular case,

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:37.040
the Don McGahn case,

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.930
I would probably file an emergency motion

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:39.860
with the Court of Appeals saying

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:41.080
that the Trump administration

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.750
has basically conceded the issue,

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:43.900
and that the Court of Appeal

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:46.960
needs to compel the
testimony of Don McGahn.

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:48.330
Now, there's sort of a pet theory

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:51.270
going around the Internet
that Chief Justice Roberts

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.740
as the presiding judge over this trial

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.830
can save a lot of these issues

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:57.660
and can rise up and can prevent

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.210
some of the obfuscation
from the Republicans.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:02.800
I would say that that theory
was dealt a serious blow

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:04.560
during the first day of the trial

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.900
when Roberts really didn't do anything

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.550
and wasn't even given some of the power

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:11.280
that the Democrats want him to receive,

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.780
and that Roberts is really just going

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:15.160
to try to disappear and
fade into the background,

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.320
like his predecessor
William Rehnquist did.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.270
Really, the only thing
that Chief Justice Roberts

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:21.670
did on the first day
when he chose to speak

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:24.000
was to dress down both sides and say

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.790
that neither is upholding the decorum

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.740
that is required for these proceedings.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:31.340
- I think it is appropriate at this point

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:35.890
for me to admonish both the House managers

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.262
and the president's counsel in equal terms

00:15:39.262 --> 00:15:41.730
to remember that they are addressing

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:44.480
the world's greatest deliberative body.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.760
One reason it has earned that title

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:50.630
is because its members
avoid speaking in a manner

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:53.180
and using language that is not conducive

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:54.670
to civil discourse.

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:56.060
- But that's sort of par for the course.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:58.500
That's what you would expect
the Chief Justice to do,

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:00.613
to fade into the background
and let the political bodies

00:16:00.613 --> 00:16:02.600
deal with the political questions.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:04.130
So, I would expect more of the same,

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:05.460
more fading into the background

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:07.900
and both sides-ing all the issues.

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:09.500
Of course, in one of
the biggest surprises,

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:12.070
senator Ted Cruz apparently singled me out

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:16.300
by intentionally misusing the
famous table-pounding quote.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:17.720
- You know, there's an old saying

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:20.470
that if you have the
facts, you bang the facts.

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.410
If you have the law, you bang the law.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:24.580
If you don't have either,
you bang the table.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:27.030
- Now, of course, I can't
be certain that Senator Cruz

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.570
is singling me out by misusing a quote

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.190
that I've used many times
on this particular channel,

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:34.370
but it really does feel like
he's turning it on its head

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:36.330
when the Democrats, whether
you liked it or not,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.060
spent hours upon hours
talking about all the evidence

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.710
that the White House is holding back,

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.970
the fact that they haven't
produced a single document

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:46.170
or allowed any White
House witnesses to testify

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:48.000
in the House or the Senate.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:48.930
Whether you liked it or not,

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:50.830
they were talking about
the substantive arguments

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:53.870
and making use of the evidence and law

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.550
that is relevant to this particular case.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.630
And of course, the craziest thing of all

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.910
is that while this is
all going on in the US,

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.600
President Trump is in Davos

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.610
where he gave a press conference

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:07.550
where he specifically bragged

00:17:07.550 --> 00:17:10.860
about how he is not giving
the Democrats any evidence

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:12.950
and that he's holding it all back.

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:14.220
- We're doing very well.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:15.820
I got to watch enough.

00:17:15.820 --> 00:17:17.730
I thought our team did a very good job,

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.860
but honestly, we have all the material.

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.260
They don't have the material.

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:22.968
- Now, if I was trying
to be charitable, here,

00:17:22.968 --> 00:17:25.180
I would say that President
Trump is just saying

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:28.130
that all of the evidence is in his favor.

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:31.220
But he specifically
uses the word material,

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.770
referring to the actual
physical things themselves

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:35.610
and not their metaphorical significance.

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.070
- We have all the material.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:38.640
They don't have the material.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.870
- So, it really does
seem like the president

00:17:40.870 --> 00:17:44.010
is bragging about obstructing Congress

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:45.320
and preventing them from getting

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:46.870
any of the relevant evidence,

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.390
and not the fact that he's just bragging

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:51.030
about how the evidence supports himself.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:53.100
It really is sort of
hilarious that the president

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.500
always seems to say the
quiet part out loud,

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:56.630
and by hilarious,

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.060
I mean that in the saddest way possible.

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:01.590
So, the Democratic argument
often boils down to saying

